  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nCurrently, an upper level trough is continues to dig into the \nnorthwestern/western CONUS with ridging over the Plains into the \nGreat Lakes and northeastern CONUS. At the surface, a low is over \nthe border of Minnesota and South Dakota with a cold front draped \nacross central Nebraska. Lee troughing remains in place with an \nincreased pressure gradient over our area, leading to breezy \nsoutherly winds especially for area west of the Kansas Turnpike. \nThese breezy conditions will persist into Monday as the upper trough \ncontinues to dig into the western CONUS, keeping an increased \npressure gradient from lee troughing over our area. Sustained winds \nwill be between 15-25 mph with gusts up to 35 mph especially in \ncentral and south-central KS. Well above normal high temperatures \nare forecast through Tuesday as increased thickness and above normal \n850 mb temperatures persist. Highs are expected to be in the upper \n80s to lower 90s for Monday and Tuesday afternoon.\n\nA pattern change is likely to start on Tuesday afternoon-Tuesday \nnight as the aforementioned upper trough over the western CONUS \npropagates east and approaches the Rockies into Wednesday. This \ntrough will bring the next round of shower and storm chances to the \nregion especially as a cold front is expected to push south through \nthe central Plains into Kansas by Tuesday morning. These rain \nchances will be possible from Tuesday evening to Thursday night \nbefore the trough continues to slide east on Friday, though the \nECMWF has a slower solution where it keeps the trough in the Rockies \nthrough Friday leading to some uncertainty on rain chances into next \nweekend. Strong to severe storms may be possible with this activity \nas effective wind shear increases combined with ample instability \nand forcing from the trough and cold front. Heavy rainfall appears \nlikely as well as PWATs approach 2\" (greater than the maximum for \nthis time of year) across our area. Details on timing, location and \nimpacts will continue to be refined as we get closer. \n\nFrom mid to late week, temperatures are forecast to become more \nseasonable as highs return to the 70s. Moving into next weekend, \nuncertainty increases on temperatures as there is still a bit of a \nspread in guidance, though the GFS has started to trend not as cold \nas before. The GFS has transitioned to a similar solution of the \nECMWF with ridging over the western CONUS into the Plains. However, \nthe GFS still has a deep upper trough over much of the eastern CONUS \nso there is still a question of how far west and south this cold air \nmakes it. On the bright side, both deterministic models are trending \ntowards high temperatures in the 50s-60s with lows in the 40s for \nnext weekend. Stay tuned!",
